<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Ghaniya Majorca</h1><p class="st-meta">1146 CE – 1203 CE (approx.) · capital Madina Mayurqa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last Almoravid state, ruled from Majorca by the Banu Ghaniya, who waged a long war against the Almohads from the Balearics to Ifriqiya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Muhammad ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-ghaniya">Ali ibn Ghaniya</a>, of an Almoravid family related to the ruling dynasty, governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> from about 1126. When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> collapsed in the 1140s, he kept the islands as an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> loyal to the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishaq-ibn-ghaniya">Ishaq ibn Muhammad</a> (c. 1155-1183) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> prospered on trade and corsair raids, and made treaties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. In 1184 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-ghaniya">Ali ibn Ghaniya</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bejaia</a> and starting a war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> that lasted half a century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1203, killing its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-ishaq">Abd Allah ibn Ishaq</a>, though the family fought on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I of Aragon</a> took Majorca in 1229, later forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-majorca">Kingdom of Majorca</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1146 CE – 1203 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.51%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1146 CE – 1203 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madina Mayurqa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Ghaniya, Ishaq ibn Muhammad, Abd Allah ibn Ishaq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Almoravid Majorca, Ghaniyid Balearics</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=banu-ghaniya-majorca" aria-label="Banu Ghaniya Majorca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4584 742 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banu Ghaniya Majorca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4799 1289h0M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4904 998h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4904 998h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=1174&amp;focus=banu-ghaniya-majorca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
